{0 Recipe: Webhook Bot}

A comprehensive cookbook for building production webhook-based bots with proper security, performance optimization, and graceful shutdown.

{1 Overview}

This recipe demonstrates how to build bots using webhooks instead of polling. Webhooks are push-based: Telegram sends updates to your server immediately, making them more efficient for production deployments.

{b What you'll learn}:
- Webhook setup and configuration
- Polling vs webhooks tradeoffs
- Security patterns (secret tokens, IP allowlisting)
- Reverse proxy configuration (Nginx/TLS)
- Graceful shutdown handling
- Request validation
- Production deployment patterns

{b When to use webhooks}:
- Production bots with high traffic
- Bots requiring minimal latency
- Cloud deployments (Kubernetes, Docker)
- Serverless/cloud function deployments
- Bots running on servers with static IPs

{1 Basic Webhook Setup}

{2 Minimal Webhook Server}

Start a basic webhook server:

{[
open Eio.Std
open Ocaml_telegram_eio

let () =
  Eio_main.run @@ fun env ->

  let token = Sys.getenv "TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN" in
  let client = Telegram.Client.create ~env ~token in

  (* Define update handler *)
  let handler update =
    match update.Telegram_generated.Gen_types.Update.message with
    | Some msg ->
        Printf.printf "Message: %Ld\n" msg.message_id;
        flush stdout
    | None -> ()
  in

  (* Run webhook server *)
  Tg.Webhook.run client
    ~secret_token:"my-secret-token-123"
    ~port:8443
    ~path:"/webhook"
    ~handler
]}

{2 Tell Telegram Where to Send Updates}

After starting your webhook server, configure Telegram:

{[
(* Set webhook URL - do this once when starting your bot *)
let setup_webhook client =
  let url = "https://example.com:8443/webhook" in
  let secret_token = "my-secret-token-123" in

  (* Using direct API call *)
  let open Telegram in
  let request = Request.create
    ~method_name:"setWebhook"
    ~parameters:[
      ("url", Param.string url);
      ("secret_token", Param.string secret_token);
      ("max_connections", Param.int 100);
    ]
    ()
  in

  match Api.call client request with
  | Ok response ->
      Printf.printf "Webhook set successfully\n"
  | Error err ->
      Printf.eprintf "Failed to set webhook: %s\n" (Error.to_string err)
]}

{2 Webhook Requirements}

Telegram requires:
- {b HTTPS}: Must use TLS (port 443, 80, 88, or 8443)
- {b Valid certificate}: Self-signed OK for testing
- {b Public URL}: Accessible from Telegram servers
- {b POST endpoint}: Accepts JSON updates

{1 Webhook vs Polling}

{2 Comparison Table}

{v
Feature              | Polling              | Webhooks
---------------------|----------------------|---------------------
Latency              | 1-30 seconds         | <1 second
Network efficiency   | Constant requests    | Only when updates arrive
Server requirements  | Any environment      | Public IP + HTTPS
Setup complexity     | Simple               | Requires TLS setup
Development          | Easy local testing   | Need tunneling (ngrok)
Production           | Higher resource use  | Lower resource use
Deployment           | Flexible             | Needs static endpoint
Cost (cloud)         | Higher (polling)     | Lower (event-driven)
v}

{2 When to Use Polling}

Choose polling for:
- {b Development}: Easy local testing without tunneling
- {b Dynamic IPs}: No static IP or domain name
- {b Simple deployment}: Quick setup without TLS
- {b Low traffic}: Few updates per minute
- {b Testing}: Rapid iteration and debugging

{2 When to Use Webhooks}

Choose webhooks for:
- {b Production}: Efficient, scalable deployments
- {b High traffic}: Many updates per second
- {b Low latency}: Immediate update delivery
- {b Cloud hosting}: AWS, GCP, Azure with load balancers
- {b Cost optimization}: Pay per request, not constant polling
- {b Serverless}: Cloud Functions, Lambda

{1 Advanced Webhook Configuration}

{2 Full Configuration Options}

Configure all webhook parameters:

{[
let config = Tg.Webhook.make
  ~port:8443
  ~path:"/webhook"
  ~secret_token:"my-secret-token-123"
  ~max_connections:100
  ~on_error:(fun err ->
    Printf.eprintf "Error: %s\n" (Telegram.Error.to_string err))
  ~ip_allowlist:Tg.Webhook.telegram_ip_ranges  (* Restrict to Telegram IPs *)
  ()

let () =
  Eio_main.run @@ fun env ->
  let token = Sys.getenv "TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN" in
  let client = Telegram.Client.create ~env ~token in

  let handler update =
    (* Process update *)
    ()
  in

  Tg.Webhook.run_with_config client config ~handler
]}

{2 Configuration Parameters}

{b port} (default: 8443):
- Port to listen on
- Telegram supports: 443, 80, 88, 8443
- Common: 8443 for bot-specific port
- Production: Often use 443 with reverse proxy

{b path} (default: "/webhook"):
- URL path for updates
- Keep it secret (e.g., "/bot<random-token>")
- Must match path in setWebhook URL

{b secret_token} (highly recommended):
- Secret string sent in X-Telegram-Bot-Api-Secret-Token header
- Validates requests are from Telegram
- Generate strong random token
- Don't commit to version control

{b max_connections} (default: 100, max: 100):
- Maximum concurrent webhook connections
- Higher = more concurrent updates
- Lower = less resource usage
- Telegram default: 40

{b on_error}:
- Callback for handling errors
- Log errors for debugging
- Optional error recovery

{b ip_allowlist}:
- Restrict requests to specific IPs
- Use Tg.Webhook.telegram_ip_ranges for Telegram IPs
- Additional security layer

{1 Security Patterns}

{2 Secret Token Validation}

Always use secret tokens:

{[
(* Generate strong secret *)
let generate_secret_token () =
  (* In production: use cryptographic random *)
  let random_bytes = Random.State.make_self_init () in
  String.init 32 (fun _ ->
    let n = Random.State.int random_bytes 62 in
    if n < 26 then char_of_int (n + 65)       (* A-Z *)
    else if n < 52 then char_of_int (n + 71)  (* a-z *)
    else char_of_int (n - 4)                  (* 0-9 *)
  )

let secret_token = generate_secret_token ()

(* Use in webhook config *)
let config = Tg.Webhook.make
  ~secret_token
  ()
]}

{2 IP Allowlisting}

Restrict to Telegram's IP ranges:

{[
(* Use built-in Telegram IP ranges *)
let config = Tg.Webhook.make
  ~port:8443
  ~path:"/webhook"
  ~secret_token:"my-secret"
  ~ip_allowlist:Tg.Webhook.telegram_ip_ranges
  ()

(* Telegram's official IP ranges (as of 2024): *)
(* - 149.154.160.0/20 *)
(* - 91.108.4.0/22 *)
]}

{2 Custom Request Validation}

Add custom validation logic:

{[
(* Rate limiting validator *)
let rate_limit_table = Hashtbl.create 100

let rate_limit_validator request =
  let open Tg.Webhook in
  let ip = request.client_addr in

  (* Check rate limit *)
  let now = Unix.time () in
  match Hashtbl.find_opt rate_limit_table ip with
  | Some (count, last_reset) ->
      if now -. last_reset > 60.0 then begin
        (* Reset after 1 minute *)
        Hashtbl.replace rate_limit_table ip (1, now);
        Accept
      end else if count > 100 then begin
        (* Too many requests *)
        Reject (Printf.sprintf "Rate limit exceeded for IP %s" ip)
      end else begin
        Hashtbl.replace rate_limit_table ip (count + 1, last_reset);
        Accept
      end
  | None ->
      Hashtbl.add rate_limit_table ip (1, now);
      Accept

let config = Tg.Webhook.make
  ~secret_token:"my-secret"
  ~custom_validator:rate_limit_validator
  ()
]}

{2 Request Header Validation}

Extract and validate request headers:

{[
(* Validate custom headers *)
let header_validator request =
  let open Tg.Webhook in

  (* Check for required custom header *)
  match List.assoc_opt "X-Custom-Auth" request.headers with
  | Some token when token = "expected-value" ->
      Accept
  | Some _ ->
      Reject "Invalid custom auth header"
  | None ->
      Reject "Missing custom auth header"

let config = Tg.Webhook.make
  ~secret_token:"telegram-secret"
  ~custom_validator:header_validator
  ()
]}

{1 Reverse Proxy Configuration}

{2 Nginx with TLS Termination}

Production setup with Nginx handling TLS:

{v
# /etc/nginx/sites-available/telegram-bot

server {
    listen 443 ssl http2;
    server_name bot.example.com;

    # TLS configuration
    ssl_certificate /etc/letsencrypt/live/bot.example.com/fullchain.pem;
    ssl_certificate_key /etc/letsencrypt/live/bot.example.com/privkey.pem;
    ssl_protocols TLSv1.2 TLSv1.3;
    ssl_ciphers HIGH:!aNULL:!MD5;

    # Webhook endpoint
    location /webhook {
        # Forward to local webhook server
        proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8080/webhook;

        # Preserve headers
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;

        # Timeouts
        proxy_connect_timeout 10s;
        proxy_send_timeout 30s;
        proxy_read_timeout 30s;

        # Disable buffering for webhooks
        proxy_buffering off;
    }
}
v}

{2 Nginx with IP Allowlist}

Add IP filtering at Nginx level:

{v
# Define Telegram IP ranges
geo $telegram_ip {
    default 0;
    149.154.160.0/20 1;
    91.108.4.0/22 1;
}

server {
    listen 443 ssl;
    server_name bot.example.com;

    ssl_certificate /etc/ssl/certs/bot.crt;
    ssl_certificate_key /etc/ssl/private/bot.key;

    location /webhook {
        # Block non-Telegram IPs
        if ($telegram_ip = 0) {
            return 403;
        }

        # Verify secret token
        if ($http_x_telegram_bot_api_secret_token != "your-secret-token") {
            return 403;
        }

        proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8080/webhook;
    }
}
v}

{2 Caddy (Simpler Alternative)}

Caddy provides automatic HTTPS:

{v
# Caddyfile

bot.example.com {
    reverse_proxy /webhook localhost:8080 {
        header_up X-Real-IP {remote_host}
        header_up X-Forwarded-For {remote_host}
    }
}
v}

{2 Application-Level IP Validation}

Handle real client IP behind proxy:

{[
(* Extract real IP from proxy headers *)
let extract_real_ip request =
  let open Tg.Webhook in

  (* Try X-Real-IP first *)
  match List.assoc_opt "X-Real-IP" request.headers with
  | Some ip -> ip
  | None ->
      (* Fallback to first X-Forwarded-For IP *)
      match List.assoc_opt "X-Forwarded-For" request.headers with
      | Some ips ->
          (match String.split_on_char ',' ips with
          | first :: _ -> String.trim first
          | [] -> request.client_addr)
      | None -> request.client_addr

(* Validate against Telegram IPs *)
let ip_validator request =
  let real_ip = extract_real_ip request in
  let open Tg.Webhook in

  (* Check if IP is in Telegram ranges *)
  let is_telegram_ip =
    List.exists (fun range ->
      Tg.Webhook.ip_in_range real_ip range
    ) Tg.Webhook.telegram_ip_ranges
  in

  if is_telegram_ip then
    Accept
  else
    Reject (Printf.sprintf "Invalid source IP: %s" real_ip)

let config = Tg.Webhook.make
  ~port:8080
  ~secret_token:"my-secret"
  ~custom_validator:ip_validator
  ()
]}

{1 Graceful Shutdown}

{2 Signal Handling with Switch}

Implement graceful shutdown:

{[
open Eio.Std
open Ocaml_telegram_eio

let () =
  Eio_main.run @@ fun env ->
  Eio.Switch.run @@ fun sw ->

  let token = Sys.getenv "TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN" in
  let client = Telegram.Client.create ~env ~token in

  (* Signal handler for graceful shutdown *)
  let shutdown _ =
    Printf.printf "Shutdown signal received, draining connections...\n";
    flush stdout;
    Eio.Switch.fail sw Exit
  in

  (* Register signal handlers *)
  Sys.set_signal Sys.sigint (Sys.Signal_handle shutdown);
  Sys.set_signal Sys.sigterm (Sys.Signal_handle shutdown);

  let handler update =
    (* Process update *)
    ()
  in

  let config = Tg.Webhook.make
    ~port:8443
    ~path:"/webhook"
    ~secret_token:"my-secret"
    ()
  in

  (* Run webhook with switch for graceful shutdown *)
  try
    Printf.printf "Starting webhook on port 8443...\n";
    flush stdout;
    Tg.Webhook.run_with_config_and_switch client config sw ~handler;
    Printf.printf "Webhook stopped gracefully\n"
  with Exit ->
    Printf.printf "Shutdown complete\n"
]}

{2 Shutdown with Timeout}

Prevent slow handlers from blocking shutdown:

{[
let handler_with_timeout env update =
  try
    (* Timeout handler after 10 seconds *)
    Eio.Time.with_timeout env#clock 10.0
      (fun () -> process_update update)
  with Eio.Time.Timeout ->
    Printf.eprintf "Handler timeout for update %Ld\n"
      update.update_id;
    flush stderr

let () =
  Eio_main.run @@ fun env ->
  Eio.Switch.run @@ fun sw ->

  let client = Telegram.Client.create ~env ~token in

  (* Shutdown signal *)
  let shutdown _ =
    Eio.Switch.fail sw Exit
  in
  Sys.set_signal Sys.sigint (Sys.Signal_handle shutdown);
  Sys.set_signal Sys.sigterm (Sys.Signal_handle shutdown);

  let config = Tg.Webhook.make ~port:8443 () in

  try
    Tg.Webhook.run_with_config_and_switch client config sw
      ~handler:(handler_with_timeout env);
    Printf.printf "Graceful shutdown complete\n"
  with Exit ->
    Printf.printf "Shutdown triggered\n"
]}

{2 Kubernetes Deployment}

Configure for Kubernetes graceful shutdown:

{v
# deployment.yaml
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: telegram-bot
spec:
  replicas: 1
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: telegram-bot
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: bot
        image: mybot:latest
        ports:
        - containerPort: 8443
        env:
        - name: TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN
          valueFrom:
            secretKeyRef:
              name: bot-secrets
              key: token
        - name: WEBHOOK_SECRET
          valueFrom:
            secretKeyRef:
              name: bot-secrets
              key: webhook-secret
        livenessProbe:
          httpGet:
            path: /health
            port: 8443
          initialDelaySeconds: 10
          periodSeconds: 30
        readinessProbe:
          httpGet:
            path: /ready
            port: 8443
          initialDelaySeconds: 5
          periodSeconds: 10
      # Allow 60 seconds for graceful shutdown
      terminationGracePeriodSeconds: 60
v}

{1 Complete Production Example}

{2 Full-Featured Webhook Bot}

Production-ready webhook bot with all features:

{[
open Eio.Std
open Ocaml_telegram_eio

(* Configuration *)
module Config = struct
  let port = int_of_string (Sys.getenv_opt "WEBHOOK_PORT" |> Option.value ~default:"8443")
  let path = Sys.getenv_opt "WEBHOOK_PATH" |> Option.value ~default:"/webhook"
  let secret_token = Sys.getenv "WEBHOOK_SECRET"
  let webhook_url = Sys.getenv "WEBHOOK_URL"
  let token = Sys.getenv "TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN"
end

(* Metrics tracking *)
module Metrics = struct
  let updates_processed = ref 0L
  let errors = ref 0L
  let start_time = ref 0.0

  let record_update () =
    updates_processed := Int64.add !updates_processed 1L

  let record_error () =
    errors := Int64.add !errors 1L

  let uptime () =
    Unix.time () -. !start_time

  let stats () =
    Printf.sprintf
      "Uptime: %.0fs | Updates: %Ld | Errors: %Ld"
      (uptime ())
      !updates_processed
      !errors
end

(* Setup webhook with Telegram *)
let setup_webhook client =
  let open Telegram in
  Printf.printf "Setting webhook to %s...\n" Config.webhook_url;
  flush stdout;

  let request = Request.create
    ~method_name:"setWebhook"
    ~parameters:[
      ("url", Param.string Config.webhook_url);
      ("secret_token", Param.string Config.secret_token);
      ("max_connections", Param.int 100);
      ("allowed_updates", Param.string_array
        [|"message"; "callback_query"; "inline_query"|]);
    ]
    ()
  in

  match Api.call client request with
  | Ok _ ->
      Printf.printf "Webhook configured successfully\n";
      flush stdout
  | Error err ->
      Printf.eprintf "Failed to set webhook: %s\n" (Error.to_string err);
      flush stderr;
      exit 1

(* Update handler *)
let handle_update update =
  Metrics.record_update ();

  match update.Telegram_generated.Gen_types.Update.message with
  | Some msg ->
      Printf.printf "Message %Ld from chat %Ld\n"
        msg.message_id
        msg.chat.id;
      flush stdout
  | None -> ()

(* Error handler *)
let on_error err =
  Metrics.record_error ();
  Printf.eprintf "Error: %s\n" (Telegram.Error.to_string err);
  flush stderr

(* Custom validator with rate limiting *)
let rate_limiter = Hashtbl.create 100

let custom_validator request =
  let open Tg.Webhook in
  let ip = request.client_addr in
  let now = Unix.time () in

  (* Rate limit: 100 requests per minute per IP *)
  match Hashtbl.find_opt rate_limiter ip with
  | Some (count, last_reset) ->
      if now -. last_reset > 60.0 then begin
        Hashtbl.replace rate_limiter ip (1, now);
        Accept
      end else if count > 100 then begin
        Reject (Printf.sprintf "Rate limit for %s" ip)
      end else begin
        Hashtbl.replace rate_limiter ip (count + 1, last_reset);
        Accept
      end
  | None ->
      Hashtbl.add rate_limiter ip (1, now);
      Accept

(* Main *)
let () =
  Eio_main.run @@ fun env ->
  Eio.Switch.run @@ fun sw ->

  Metrics.start_time := Unix.time ();

  (* Create client *)
  let client = Telegram.Client.create ~env ~token:Config.token in

  (* Setup webhook *)
  setup_webhook client;

  (* Signal handling *)
  let shutdown _ =
    Printf.printf "\nShutdown signal received\n";
    Printf.printf "Stats: %s\n" (Metrics.stats ());
    flush stdout;
    Eio.Switch.fail sw Exit
  in
  Sys.set_signal Sys.sigint (Sys.Signal_handle shutdown);
  Sys.set_signal Sys.sigterm (Sys.Signal_handle shutdown);

  (* Webhook configuration *)
  let config = Tg.Webhook.make
    ~port:Config.port
    ~path:Config.path
    ~secret_token:Config.secret_token
    ~max_connections:100
    ~on_error
    ~ip_allowlist:Tg.Webhook.telegram_ip_ranges
    ~custom_validator
    ()
  in

  (* Handler with timeout *)
  let handler_with_timeout update =
    try
      Eio.Time.with_timeout env#clock 10.0
        (fun () -> handle_update update)
    with Eio.Time.Timeout ->
      Printf.eprintf "Handler timeout\n";
      flush stderr
  in

  (* Run webhook *)
  try
    Printf.printf "Starting webhook on port %d...\n" Config.port;
    flush stdout;

    Tg.Webhook.run_with_config_and_switch client config sw
      ~handler:handler_with_timeout;

    Printf.printf "Webhook stopped gracefully\n";
    Printf.printf "Final stats: %s\n" (Metrics.stats ())
  with Exit ->
    Printf.printf "Shutdown complete\n"
]}

{1 Best Practices}

{2 Security}

- {b Always use secret token}: Generate strong random token
- {b IP allowlist}: Use Telegram's IP ranges
- {b HTTPS required}: Use valid TLS certificate
- {b Hide webhook path}: Use random/secret path
- {b Rate limiting}: Protect against abuse
- {b Reverse proxy}: Nginx/Caddy for TLS termination

{2 Performance}

- {b Concurrent processing}: Let Eio handle concurrency
- {b max_connections}: Tune based on server capacity
- {b Handler timeouts}: Prevent slow requests blocking shutdown
- {b Async operations}: Use Eio fibers for I/O
- {b Connection pooling}: Reuse HTTP connections

{2 Reliability}

- {b Graceful shutdown}: Use Eio switches
- {b Error handling}: Log and track errors
- {b Health checks}: Implement liveness/readiness probes
- {b Monitoring}: Track metrics (updates, errors, uptime)
- {b Logging}: Structured logging for debugging

{2 Deployment}

- {b Environment variables}: Never hardcode secrets
- {b Docker/Kubernetes}: Use orchestration for scaling
- {b Load balancing}: Distribute across multiple instances
- {b Auto-scaling}: Scale based on traffic
- {b Blue-green deployment}: Zero-downtime updates

{2 Development}

- {b Local testing}: Use ngrok or localtunnel for HTTPS
- {b Development mode}: Use polling for local development
- {b Staging environment}: Test webhooks before production
- {b Monitoring}: Set up logging and metrics early

{1 Troubleshooting}

{2 Webhook Not Receiving Updates}

Check:
1. Webhook URL is publicly accessible
2. HTTPS is configured correctly
3. Port is one of: 443, 80, 88, 8443
4. Secret token matches in setWebhook and config
5. Check Telegram's webhook info: getWebhookInfo

{2 Certificate Issues}

For self-signed certificates:
{[
(* Upload certificate when setting webhook *)
let request = Request.create
  ~method_name:"setWebhook"
  ~parameters:[
    ("url", Param.string url);
    ("certificate", Param.file_upload cert_path);
  ]
  ()
]}

{2 IP Validation Failing}

Behind reverse proxy:
- Use X-Real-IP or X-Forwarded-For headers
- Validate IP at application level
- Check proxy configuration

{2 Shutdown Taking Too Long}

Solutions:
- Add request timeouts to handlers
- Lower max_connections
- Set proxy timeouts
- Implement handler cancellation

